  m_NavMeshLayer: 0
  m_StaticEditorFlags: 0
  m_IsActive: 1
--- !u!114 &1113022268
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1113022267}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ebebb1285e41b2031b53d4870ed430db, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Gridlock.Runtime::Gridlock.Guide.GuideTour
  steps:
  - id: welcome
    title: Welcome to the lot
    text: Welcome to the parking lot. In the next two minutes, you will see how programs
      in a computer can get completely stuck, and how the system sets them free.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: fd694b39ad5a2726c86cd6e0807081fa, type: 3}
  - id: environment
    title: Cars and bays
    text: Think of this lot as a computer. Each car is a process, a program that
      wants to run. Each parking bay is a resource, like a printer or a file. A bay
      holds only one car at a time.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 4d6205320ca3debc59d0ad1f4a45d64d, type: 3}
  - id: allocate
    title: Allocation
    text: Watch the cars ask for bays. The bays are free, so the system hands them
      out. Giving a process the resource it asks for is called allocation.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: dc5e8901b1207fe339ddd5efe4908511, type: 3}
  - id: wait
    title: Hold and wait
    text: Now car A wants to move into bay P2. But B is parked there, so A must wait.
      And while it waits, it keeps its own bay. This is called hold and wait.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: bd78f09c56e34c0d48c3595cd9cf0362, type: 3}
  - id: circle
    title: A circle of waiting
    text: B wants C's bay. C wants D's bay. And D wants A's bay. Every car now waits
      for the next one, in a circle. No one can ever move. This is a deadlock.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 5203936c32518504eb9e9f056d88a318, type: 3}
  - id: detect
    title: Detecting the deadlock
    text: The system spots it by drawing who waits for whom. These arrows close into
      a loop, from A to B to C to D, and back to A. A loop means deadlock.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: cc83d8e8335dcfe0b8b75d19fdd2cf4c, type: 3}
  - id: tow
    title: Your turn
    text: Your turn. To break the loop, the system can take a resource away by force.
      Select any red car, then press Tow car on the guide screen.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 1d90f8fd8061cf22a990ec55e487854d, type: 3}
  - id: tow_auto
    title: Your turn
    text: Let's tow car D together.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 5922e337ca630b1b99975149d59f8938, type: 3}
  - id: unwind
    title: The jam clears
    text: The towed car leaves and frees its bay. The next car moves in and frees
      another bay, and the whole jam unwinds. This is called recovery by preemption.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 13940310dea150135a473ee057635fa4, type: 3}
  - id: outro
    title: 'Even better: avoid it'
    text: Better still is to avoid deadlock before it happens. The Banker's Algorithm
      grants a request only if everyone can still finish. Try it in Banker mode.
      Now the lot is yours. Select a car, then a bay, and experiment.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 1ffa03072aa9b512396c379e144653a2, type: 3}
  marker: {fileID: 543056614}
  desktopScreen: {fileID: 30845390}
  xrScreen: {fileID: 1425496721}
  xrMode: {fileID: 667742936}
  desktopCamera: {fileID: 808265113}
  simulation: {fileID: 441358384}
  overlay: {fileID: 441358387}
  interaction: {fileID: 441358386}
  cityAudio: {fileID: 441358385}
  towWaitSeconds: 15
